# A pane showing Settings has no composer, so everything that would aim at
# one says so instead of staging it out of sight.
#
# The failure this guards: a drop or a keystroke on the Settings pane going
# into the NEW-THREAD composer (current_composer_target's kickoff fallback),
# where the reader finds it later in a box they never typed into. And in a
# split, the other pane must still take its own input normally.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1440,"window_height":900,"open_tabs":["t2","t6"],"active_tab":"t2","split_open":true,"split_ratio":0.5,"split_panes":["t2","t6"],"split_focused_pane":0,"theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_WIN_W=1440 HANABI_WIN_H=900 HANABI_OPEN=t2 HANABI_SPLIT=t6
require_thread t2
require_thread t6
expect_panes t2 t6 0

# A draft in the LEFT pane, which nothing below may touch.
click_ui composer_reply_input
wait_frames 10
expect_panes t2 t6 0
type "the other pane's own words"
wait_frames 8
expect_input_text composer_reply_input "the other pane's own words"

# Focus the RIGHT pane, so Settings takes THAT one: a surface lands in the
# pane the reader is in.
click_ui composer_reply_input_other
wait_frames 10
expect_panes t2 t6 1
click_ui sb_settings
wait_frames 24
expect_text "SETTINGS"

# Typing with no field focused, which normally SEEDS the composer: on a
# surface pane it must go nowhere at all -- not into the new-thread
# composer, where the reader would find it later in a box they never typed
# into.
# Typing goes to the settings search field, and that is correct: the
# settings pane takes the caret when it opens (settings_system.h grabs it
# every frame while the search zone is focused), so keystrokes filter the
# settings list -- the surface is a place you type INTO, just not a
# conversation.
#
# What this asserts is the guarantee that matters: those keystrokes do not
# reach any COMPOSER. The seed path's own refusal (a keystroke with no
# field focused, on a surface pane) cannot be reached from a script while
# the pane holds the caret; it is in the code as the backstop for a surface
# that does not grab focus, and it is NOT proven here.
# (The left pane's composer is the only one now, so it carries the plain
# name -- the surface pane has none.)
type "filtering the settings"
wait_frames 16
expect_input_text composer_reply_input "the other pane's own words"

# The conversation pane still takes its own input: clicking its composer
# moves the caret out of the settings search field, and typing extends the
# draft that was there.
click_ui composer_reply_input
wait_frames 16
type " and more"
wait_frames 20
expect_input_text composer_reply_input "the other pane's own words and more"
